KNOWNBUG: instantiating unordered_set<K>::insert first (materializing
allocator<_Hash_node<K,..>> rebinds) makes the LATER vector<K>
instantiation fail to resolve _Vector_base's _Tp_alloc_type/pointer
("symbol '_Tp_alloc_type' is unknown") -- vector machinery left
incomplete, v[0] reads garbage.  vector-first order works.  Found
dog-fooding src/util/find_symbols.cpp (unordered_set<irep_idt> +
std::vector<irep_idt>).  Flip to CORE when fixed.

DIAGNOSIS 2026-07-17: the failing name is `__gnu_cxx::__alloc_traits<
_Alloc>::template rebind<_Tp>::other` while instantiating
_Vector_base<K>.  The scope-restricted lookup of `rebind` in the
__alloc_traits<allocator<K>,K> instance scope finds NOTHING: the
instance is fully elaborated (19 components, complete) but its member
TEMPLATE `rebind` was never registered in its scope (member templates
are only registered when used during the class's own instantiation --
the _Hash_node-flavoured __alloc_traits instance has its rebind because
unordered_set used it then).  The recursive fallback then scans the
whole program for `rebind` and reports "template scope 'rebind' is
ambiguous" -- with the CORRECT candidate not in the set.  Two sound
improvements landed (tag-aware scope-of-T candidate filter;
elaborate-and-retry on empty lookup) but neither registers the missing
member template.  REAL FIX: `T::template name<args>` lookup must fall
back to T's PRIMARY TEMPLATE's scope with the instance's template map
([basic.lookup.qual], [temp.names]/3, [temp.mem]), or member templates
must be registered when a class instance is completed.

See cpp17_member_template_completed_instance for the sharpened
diagnosis (member class templates lack scope entries until used;
registration must happen at instance-body conversion).

UPDATE 2026-07-17 night: the rebind ambiguity and the _Tp_alloc_type
failures are FIXED (cpp17_member_template_completed_instance is CORE;
per-member recovery in typecheck_compound_body) and
src/util/find_symbols.cpp dog-foods CLEAN.  This test's residual:
vector<K>::push_back's body conversion fails through a SILENT
qualified-lookup throw -- `type` in std::tag-enable_if<0,void>
([temp.deduct]/8 SFINAE shape): _S_use_relocate's enable_if evaluates
FALSE here even though the stdlib model overrides
_S_nothrow_relocate/_S_use_relocate to true (the override applies to
the function body, not this constexpr use in a return type), so the
push_back body's _M_realloc_append call fails substitution and the
member is dropped.  Next lead: make the model override also cover the
constexpr evaluation, or model push_back/_M_realloc_append directly.

UPDATE 2026-07-18: residual sharpened into
cpp17_push_back_after_unordered_set_decl -- a bare unordered_set<K>*
declaration (no object, no insert) already drops vector<K>::push_back.
Note _Hash_node also uses __aligned_membuf, so
cpp11_alignas_member_layout applies to the value storage here too.
